# Approach A of docs/development/rfc-linux-x64-build-host.md: build the x86_64 Linux
# Datadog.Tracer.Native / Datadog.Profiler.Native artifacts on a plain, current Ubuntu host
# while still producing a binary whose glibc floor matches what compiling inside a real
# CentOS 7 container produces today (GLIBC_2.17). Modeled directly on the musl cross-toolchain
# file this repo already ships and runs in production for the "Universal" build
# (Universal.cmake.x86_64) - same mechanism, re-pointed at glibc instead of musl.
#
# Unlike Universal.cmake.x86_64, this file deliberately does NOT set CMAKE_SYSROOT: doing so
# would also redirect the *compiler's* header search into the sysroot, and we want to keep
# compiling C against the build host's own modern glibc headers (needed for -std=c++20 -
# CentOS 7's own headers predate that). This is safe for glibc's OWN symbol-version ceiling,
# because that's a link-time-only property (which .so the linker resolves undefined symbols
# against, via that .so's .gnu.version_d section) - it is not affected by which glibc headers
# were used to compile. The one known glibc-side exception - functions whose
# compiler-generated call target differs depending on which glibc header version compiled
# the call (the historic stat/fstat/lstat -> __xstat/__fxstat/__lxstat indirection) - is
# handled by linking in the existing glibc-compat shim... except that shim was tried here and
# reverted: its strerror_r override hard-conflicts with real glibc's _GNU_SOURCE-declared
# signature (see tracer/src/Datadog.Tracer.Native/CMakeLists.txt's comment). Empirically,
# stat/fstat/lstat resolve correctly on this path without it regardless (see the RFC).
#
# The SAME "compile with modern headers is fine" reasoning does NOT hold for C++: libstdc++
# is not just a symbol-version target, its HEADERS contain inlined implementations (e.g.
# std::condition_variable::wait_until) that call glibc functions newer than 2.17 directly
# (__libc_single_threaded, __cxa_thread_atexit_impl, pthread_cond_clockwait - all verified as
# hard "undefined symbol" link failures in CI, not just version mismatches) whenever a
# modern GCC's own headers/libstdc++.a are used - which is what clang finds by default via
# its own GCC-installation auto-detection on any current Ubuntu host. So both the C++ HEADER
# search and the libstdc++.a/libgcc.a used at link time are redirected below to a harvested
# devtoolset-11 (Red Hat's own GCC 11, rebuilt specifically to still target RHEL7's glibc
# floor - not a coincidence that it shares a GCC major version with a typical modern host's
# system GCC, but built to assume nothing newer than glibc 2.17 is present). devtoolset-11 is
# also the newest devtoolset CentOS 7 ever shipped (there is no devtoolset-12) and already a
# pipeline dependency elsewhere (centos7.build.dockerfile bootstraps clang-16 with it).
#
# So this file changes two things: where the COMPILER looks for C++ standard library headers
# (-cxx-isystem, C headers are untouched - see above), and where the LINKER looks for
# libraries/the dynamic-linker/interpreter (library search paths, glibc's own sysroot, and
# devtoolset-11's libstdc++.a/libgcc.a) - pointing both at a frozen, harvested sysroot instead
# of the Ubuntu host's own modern glibc/GCC. See glibc217-sysroot.harvest.dockerfile for how
# /sysroot/x86_64-glibc217 (including its devtoolset11/ subdirectory) gets populated
# (harvested once, out-of-band, from a real centos:7 container - never rebuilt per-CI-job).

# Defaults to the path ubuntu.dockerfile's `COPY --from=glibc217-harvest` populates, which is
# the only place this build normally runs. Overridable purely so a one-off local repro doesn't
# require editing this tracked file (which is easy to forget to revert, and produces a
# confusing diff):
#   cmake -DGLIBC217_SYSROOT=/path/to/harvested/sysroot ...
# The harvested tarball extracts straight into such a directory (its members are ./usr/lib64,
# ./lib64, ./devtoolset11), so `mkdir sysroot && tar xzf glibc217-sysroot-x86_64.tar.gz -C
# sysroot` then pointing this at it is enough - no sudo and no writing to /sysroot needed.
set(GLIBC217_SYSROOT "/sysroot/x86_64-glibc217" CACHE PATH
    "Location of the harvested glibc 2.17 + devtoolset-11 sysroot")
set(glibc217_sysroot ${GLIBC217_SYSROOT})

# try_compile() (which project()/enable_language run for compiler ABI detection) generates a
# throwaway sub-project that re-includes this toolchain file but does NOT inherit the outer
# cache, so GLIBC217_SYSROOT would revert to its default there and the FATAL_ERROR below would
# fire from inside the ABI check - "CMAKE_C_COMPILER not set, after EnableLanguage", which
# gives no hint about the real cause. Forwarding it is the documented fix. Only matters when
# the default is overridden, which is why the in-Docker path never hit this.
list(APPEND CMAKE_TRY_COMPILE_PLATFORM_VARIABLES GLIBC217_SYSROOT)
set(devtoolset11_dir ${glibc217_sysroot}/devtoolset11)

if (NOT EXISTS ${glibc217_sysroot})
    message(FATAL_ERROR "USE_GLIBC217_SYSROOT is set but ${glibc217_sysroot} does not exist. "
                         "The Docker image should have extracted the harvested glibc 2.17 "
                         "sysroot tarball there - see docs/development/rfc-linux-x64-build-host.md. "
                         "To build outside that image, point -DGLIBC217_SYSROOT= at a local copy.")
endif()

if (NOT EXISTS ${devtoolset11_dir})
    message(FATAL_ERROR "USE_GLIBC217_SYSROOT is set but ${devtoolset11_dir} does not exist. "
                         "The harvested sysroot must also include devtoolset-11's "
                         "libstdc++/libgcc and C++ headers - see "
                         "docs/development/rfc-linux-x64-build-host.md.")
endif()

# Selecting this toolchain file IS the opt-in, so USE_GLIBC217_SYSROOT is defined HERE rather
# than passed as a second -D that callers would have to keep in sync with -DCMAKE_TOOLCHAIN_FILE=
# (Build.cs deliberately passes only the latter). A plain set() is enough and reaches the
# subdirectories that gate on it: CMake includes this file from CMakeSystem.cmake during the
# top-level project() call, so these variables land in the top-level directory scope and are
# inherited by add_subdirectory(tracer)/add_subdirectory(profiler).
#
# Without it, the two things the subdirectory CMakeLists gate on this variable for both silently
# don't happen: glibc217-compat never gets built or linked (verified: "ld.lld-16: error:
# undefined symbol: __libc_single_threaded, referenced by atomicity.h:52 ... referenced 3322 more
# times" when linking Datadog.Profiler.Native.so - the reference our own sys/single_threaded.h
# stub deliberately creates, with nothing left to back it), and the redundant -stdlib=libstdc++
# stays on the command line, warning "argument unused during compilation" on every single file.
set(USE_GLIBC217_SYSROOT ON)

# UNIVERSAL and USE_GLIBC217_SYSROOT are mutually exclusive: the Universal build targets musl
# (that's the whole point of the glibc-compat shim it pulls in - see FindGlibcCompat.cmake),
# while this file targets a frozen glibc. The native loader and wrapper cannot be both at once.
#
# Root CMakeLists.txt declares UNIVERSAL with option(... ON), so the mere DEFAULT would
# otherwise silently opt this build into the musl path. Seeding the cache here - before that
# option() runs, since this file is included from project() - makes the natural
# "-DCMAKE_TOOLCHAIN_FILE=<this file>" configure self-consistent without callers having to
# also remember -DUNIVERSAL=OFF (which is what CI's CompileNativeWrapper already passes
# explicitly). Verified: with UNIVERSAL left at its ON default, building the native-loader
# target under this toolchain fails to compile outright -
# "shared/src/glibc-compat/glibc-compat.c:111:5: error: conflicting types for 'strerror_r'",
# because that shim defines musl's XSI int-returning strerror_r while real glibc here declares
# the _GNU_SOURCE char*-returning one. No FORCE, so an explicit -DUNIVERSAL=ON on the command
# line still wins - and root CMakeLists.txt turns that contradiction into a hard error rather
# than letting it reach that confusing compile failure.
set(UNIVERSAL OFF CACHE BOOL "Build as Universal binary")

set(CMAKE_SYSTEM_NAME Linux)
set(CMAKE_SYSTEM_PROCESSOR x86_64)
set(triple x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu)
set(CMAKE_C_COMPILER_TARGET ${triple})
set(CMAKE_CXX_COMPILER_TARGET ${triple})

# CMAKE_CURRENT_LIST_DIR (this file's own directory), not DOTNET_TRACER_REPO_ROOT_PATH/
# CMAKE_SOURCE_DIR: toolchain files are processed before project()/the root CMakeLists.txt
# body runs, so those aren't reliably set yet here.
get_filename_component(glibc217_compat_include_dir "${CMAKE_CURRENT_LIST_DIR}/../../shared/src/glibc217-compat" ABSOLUTE)

# -isystem shadows glibc's real <sys/single_threaded.h> (introduced in glibc 2.32) with our
# own stub - see shared/src/glibc217-compat/glibc217-compat.c for the full story: libstdc++'s
# <ext/atomicity.h> does `#if __has_include(<sys/single_threaded.h>)`, re-evaluated fresh on
# every file that includes it against whatever C headers are reachable at that moment - since
# C header search isn't redirected below (only C++ - see the next comment for why that's
# normally fine), the build host's own modern glibc satisfies that check, pulling in
# __libc_single_threaded, which doesn't exist in glibc 2.17 (verified in CI: "undefined
# symbol: __libc_single_threaded", from ordinary std::string/std::shared_ptr usage - not
# something the earlier empirical verification below happened to exercise). Must come before
# the devtoolset-11 redirect below is irrelevant to ordering - -isystem and -cxx-isystem are
# separate search lists - but verified together as one flag set regardless.
#
# -nostdinc++ + -cxx-isystem redirect the C++ standard library header search to
# devtoolset-11's (main dir + the arch-specific subdir, which has bits/c++config.h) - C
# headers are untouched, still resolved from the Ubuntu host's own modern glibc (see the
# file comment for why that's safe). Without this, functions like
# std::condition_variable::wait_until get their glibc-version-dependent implementation
# inlined from whichever C++ headers compiled the call, regardless of which libstdc++.a is
# linked (verified in CI: this was still broken with only the *library* redirected below).
set(CMAKE_CXX_FLAGS_INIT "-isystem ${glibc217_compat_include_dir} -nostdinc++ -cxx-isystem ${devtoolset11_dir}/include/11 -cxx-isystem ${devtoolset11_dir}/include/11/x86_64-redhat-linux")

# -Wl,--sysroot= is required, not optional: glibc's own /usr/lib64/libc.so, libpthread.so,
# etc. are GNU ld linker SCRIPTS (text files containing e.g.
# "GROUP ( /lib64/libpthread-2.17.so /lib64/libpthread_nonshared.a ... )"), and those paths
# are ABSOLUTE, as shipped on the source CentOS 7 system. Without --sysroot telling the
# LINKER (not the compiler - this is passed via -Wl, specifically so it never touches header
# search) to treat absolute paths as sysroot-relative, the linker resolves them against the
# real build-host filesystem instead and fails outright (verified: `ld.lld: error: cannot
# open /lib64/libpthread.so.0: No such file or directory` without this flag).
#
# -B tells the compiler driver to also look here for crt*.o startup objects, so the ones
# glibc itself provides (crt1.o/crti.o/crtn.o) come from the frozen sysroot rather than the
# host's own glibc; the devtoolset-11 lib dir is also added to -B/-L so -static-libstdc++/
# -static-libgcc pick up ITS libstdc++.a/libgcc.a instead of whatever GCC clang would
# otherwise auto-detect on the build host. -rpath-link points the linker's own transitive
# search at the same two places.
#
# Deliberately NOT set here: -Wl,--dynamic-linker. Pointing the ELF interpreter at the
# sysroot's own ld.so looks right by symmetry with everything else above, but it bakes an
# ABSOLUTE BUILD-HOST path into PT_INTERP, and that path exists only on the machine that did
# the linking - not on the target. It also isn't needed: /lib64/ld-linux-x86-64.so.2 (clang's
# default for this triple) is the x86_64 psABI-mandated interpreter path, byte-identical on
# CentOS 7 and on any modern distro, so the default is already the portable answer.
#
# This is latent rather than obvious because PT_INTERP only exists in EXECUTABLES: the
# tracer/profiler .so targets silently ignored the flag, so only the GoogleTest executables
# ever tripped over it. And they trip hard - gtest_discover_tests RUNS the freshly linked test
# binary at BUILD time to enumerate test cases, so this surfaced as a build failure, not a
# test failure: "Datadog.Profiler.Native.Tests: <sysroot>/lib64/ld-linux-x86-64.so.2: version
# `GLIBC_2.35' not found (required by /lib/x86_64-linux-gnu/libc.so.6)". Note what that error
# actually says - the pinned glibc-2.17 ld.so was found and started, then went on to load the
# HOST's modern libc.so.6 (its own default search path, not the sysroot's), and a 2.17 loader
# cannot satisfy a 2.36 libc. Leaving PT_INTERP at the default gives the matched
# loader/libc pair on whatever machine runs the binary, old or new, which is the entire goal.
#
# This whole flag set was verified empirically (not just reasoned about): compiling a test
# program exercising stat/fstat/lstat/pow/ceil/malloc/free/dlopen AND
# std::mutex/std::condition_variable::wait_until/thread_local-with-destructor/std::atomic
# (to specifically exercise __libc_single_threaded/__cxa_thread_atexit_impl/
# pthread_cond_clockwait) with a current Ubuntu's own modern C++ headers redirected to a
# harvested devtoolset-11, and linking with exactly these flags against a sysroot harvested
# per glibc217-sysroot.harvest.dockerfile, produced a clean link with `nm
# --with-symbol-versions` showing a max requirement of exactly GLIBC_2.17, and none of the
# three problem symbols referenced at all. The same source linked normally (no sysroot, no
# devtoolset-11 redirect) against a stock Ubuntu 22.04 fails to link outright on those three
# undefined symbols, and separately pulls in GLIBC_2.34 for the parts that do resolve. See
# docs/development/rfc-linux-x64-build-host.md for the full comparison.
# -Wl,--no-dependent-libraries: lld (unlike bfd/gold, which don't have this feature at all -
# why this was never an issue on the existing centos7 build) honors MSVC-style
# #pragma comment(lib, "X") directives recorded in object files as ELF "dependent library"
# specifiers, even on Linux - clang parses the pragma regardless of target OS. Some vendored
# CoreCLR PAL header (Windows-originating source) has one for mscoree.lib, a Windows-only
# library that obviously doesn't exist here (verified in CI: "ld.lld: error: unable to find
# library from dependent library specifier: mscoree.lib", across several *.cpp.o in
# Datadog.Tracer.Native.Tests). This directive was always latent in the code; explicitly
# selecting lld below (matching the Universal build's own precedent) is what exposes it,
# since lld defaults this feature to enabled for ELF targets. Disabling it here doesn't lose
# anything real - nothing in this codebase actually wants MSVC's implicit-autolink behavior.
#
# -Wno-unused-command-line-argument is on the LINK flags only, and only silences a false
# positive that CMake's design forces on us: CMake appends CMAKE_CXX_FLAGS to the link command
# line as well as to compile lines, so the compile-only -nostdinc++ above rides along to a
# link-only clang invocation, which then quite correctly reports "argument unused during
# compilation: '-nostdinc++'" - once per linked target, with nothing wrong to fix. Scoped to the
# linker flags rather than added to CMAKE_CXX_FLAGS_INIT deliberately: real unused-argument
# warnings during actual COMPILATION still surface normally. (Universal.cmake.x86_64 solves the
# same problem with the blunter -Qunused-arguments in its shared compile+link flags.)
set(glibc217_link_flags "-fuse-ld=lld-16 -Wno-unused-command-line-argument -Wl,--no-dependent-libraries -static-libgcc -static-libstdc++ -B${devtoolset11_dir}/lib -L${devtoolset11_dir}/lib -Wl,--sysroot=${glibc217_sysroot} -B${glibc217_sysroot}/usr/lib64 -B${glibc217_sysroot}/lib64 -L${glibc217_sysroot}/usr/lib64 -L${glibc217_sysroot}/lib64 -Wl,-rpath-link=${glibc217_sysroot}/lib64:${glibc217_sysroot}/usr/lib64")

set(CMAKE_EXE_LINKER_FLAGS_INIT "${glibc217_link_flags}")
set(CMAKE_SHARED_LINKER_FLAGS_INIT "${glibc217_link_flags}")

# -fuse-ld=lld-16 (versioned, matching ubuntu.dockerfile's llvm.sh-installed ld.lld-16 -
# there's no bare `lld` symlink, only clang/clang++/clang-tidy/run-clang-tidy are symlinked
# to unversioned names) is pinned explicitly, matching this repo's own Universal.cmake.x86_64
# precedent of pinning binutils rather than trusting the build host's ambient default (which
# varies by image/distro between ld.bfd/ld.gold/lld). The empirical verification above was
# run with plain `lld` from Ubuntu 22.04's own default package, which resolves the same way.

# To debug what the linker actually resolves against, append -Wl,-verbose to the flags above
# and inspect the "attempt to open ..." trace, or just re-run
# ValidateNativeTracerGlibcCompatibility/ValidateNativeProfilerGlibcCompatibility (nm
# --with-symbol-versions) against the produced .so - that's the authoritative check.
